response:
 This is a basic problem in number theory. We are looking for a number \( n \) that satisfies the following congruences: \[
\begin{cases}
n \equiv 1 \pmod{3} \\
n \equiv 1 \pmod{4} \\
n \equiv 4 \pmod{5}
\end{cases}
\] We can combine the first two congruences to get \( n = 3k + 1 \) for some integer \( k \), and then further simplify it to \( n = 12m + 1 \) because 12 is the least common multiple (LCM) of 3 and 4. Now we need to find \( m \) such that \( 12m + 1 \equiv 4 \pmod{5} \). Simplifying this, we get \( 12m \equiv 3 \pmod{5} \), and since \( 12 \equiv 2 \pmod{5} \), it further simplifies to \( 2m \equiv 3 \pmod{5} \). Multiplying both sides by 3 (the modular inverse of 2 modulo 5), we get \( m \equiv 4 \pmod{5} \). So \( m = 5p + 4 \) for some integer \( p \). Substituting \( m \) back into the expression for \( n \), we get \( n = 12(5p + 4) + 1 = 60p + 49 \). The smallest positive integer \( n \) occurs when \( p = 0 \), so \( n = 49 \). </think> <answer> 49 </answer>

